ince the user hasn\'t checked that yet, maybe they should look into the deployment\'s events again. Alternatively, check the node\'s logs for any issues with pulling the image. But the available tools are limited. The user can use get_metrics to check for any metrics related to image pulls. For example, a Prometheus query like "container_image_pull_failures" or similar. But the exact metric name depends on the setup. Alternatively, check the pod\'s status for any errors. Since there are no pods, maybe the deployment is failing to create them. The next step would be to check the deployment\'s status and events again, or check the image pull status. Since the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, they can run "kubectl describe deployment search" again to see if there are any events indicating image pull errors. Wait, they already did that. The previous describe deployment might not have shown the events. Alternatively, check the logs of the deployment\'s pods, but since there are no pods, maybe the issue is that the image can\'t be pulled. So, the user could try to check if the image exists in the registry. But without access to the registry, that\'s hard. Alternatively, check the image pull secret in the namespace. But how? The user could check the secrets in the namespace. So, maybe the next step is to list the secrets in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if there\'s a pull secret for the private registry. The tool exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with "kubectl get secrets -n hotel-reservation". If there\'s a secret that\'s supposed to be used for pulling the image, but it\'s missing or misconfigured, that could be the root cause. So, the user should check the secrets in the namespace. That makes sense. So the next tool call would be to get the secrets in the hotel-reservation namespace.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The deployment\'s image is `yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest`, but no pods are running. To determine if the image pull is failing (e.g., due to missing secrets or registry issues), I need to check the secrets in the namespace. If the image is from a private registry, the deployment might lack the necessary credentials to pull it.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get secrets -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 878, 'prompt_tokens': 3618, 'total_tokens': 4496, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcaa-e0b4-7b60-bd63-6afb34687a93-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3618, 'output_tokens': 878, 'total_tokens': 4496}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d71f084c-3228-4b6b-8427-f3c37fa1d703'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the "search" service in the hotel-reservation namespace isn\'t running. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments and found that the search deployment has 0/1 pods ready. They looked at the pods and found none exist, then checked the deployment details. The image specified is yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest. Now, they checked the secrets in the namespace.\n\nThe next step is to see if there are any secrets related to pulling the image. If the image is from a private registry, the deployment would need a secret with the credentials. The user might need to check if the secret exists and if it\'s correctly configured. If there\'s no secret, that could be why the image isn\'t pulling, leading to the pods not starting. Alternatively, maybe the secret is named differently or has the wrong credentials. So, checking the secrets is a logical next step to verify if that\'s the issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get secrets -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8b894ee6de0c3ab7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 234, 'prompt_tokens': 4639, 'total_tokens': 4873, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcab-0984-7353-9a68-4b122d3e5a40-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get secrets -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8b894ee6de0c3ab7', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4639, 'output_tokens': 234, 'total_tokens': 4873}), ToolMessage(content='', id='88b60057-7855-4772-9ad0-54991616f602', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8b894ee6de0c3ab7'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='cfc9a886-51b2-47fd-bb5c-00e1c53aa887'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the "search" service in the hotel-reservation namespace isn\'t running. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments and found that the "search" deployment has 0/1 pods ready. They looked at the pod details and found no pods exist, then checked the deployment\'s container image, which is yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest. Then they checked the secrets in the namespace but didn\'t find any relevant ones.\n\nHmm, the next step is to check if the image pull is failing. The user mentioned that if the image is from a private registry, the deployment might lack the necessary credentials. But when they ran "kubectl get secrets -n hotel-reservation", there were no secrets listed. Wait, maybe the secret is named differently or isn\'t in the namespace? Or perhaps the image is from a public registry, but there\'s another issue.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the deployment\'s image pull policy is set to "IfNotPresent", and the image isn\'t available locally. Or maybe there\'s a typo in the image name. The user should check the image pull policy and verify the image exists. Also, checking the events for the deployment could provide more clues. Let me think, the next tool to use would be to get the events related to the deployment. That way, they can see if there are any errors during the pod creation, like image pull errors or other issues. So the command would be "kubectl describe deployment search -n hotel-reservation" again to check the events section. Wait, they already did that earlier. Let me check the previous steps.\n\nWait, in the previous steps, when they ran "kubectl describe deployment search -n hotel-reservation", maybe the events weren\'t shown. But the user hasn\'t checked the events yet. So the next step is to look into the events associated with the deployment to find out why the pods aren\'t starting. That would give more specific error messages, like "Failed to pull image" or "No available container in image". So the tool call should be to describe the deployment again to get the events. Alternatively, use "kubectl get events -n hotel-reservation" to see recent events. But since the focus is on the search deployment, checking the deployment\'s own events would be more targeted. So the next tool call should be "kubectl describe deployment search -n hotel-reservation" to look at the events section. But the user already did that. Wait, maybe they didn\'t check the events in the previous describe. Let me check the previous tool response. The last tool call was "kubectl get deployment search -n hotel-reservation -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}\'" which returned the image name. Then they checked secrets and found none. So the next step is to check the events for the deployment. So the correct tool call would be "kubectl describe deployment search -n hotel-reservation" again to look at the Events section. That would show any errors related to pulling the image or other issues. Alternatively, check the pod logs if there are any pods, but since there are no pods, maybe the events are the way to go. So the next tool is to describe the deployment to get events.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The deployment\'s image is from a private registry (`yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest`), but no secrets were found in the namespace. To confirm if the image pull is failing due to missing credentials, I need to check the deployment\'s events for explicit error messages (e.g., "Failed to pull image"). This will directly indicate if the issue is related to authentication or image availability.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe deployment search -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 797, 'prompt_tokens': 3824, 'total_tokens': 4621, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcab-175b-7353-9934-d63001794abe-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3824, 'output_tokens': 797, 'total_tokens': 4621}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c3df3bb4-0acd-4d9a-9b27-ec49c747ea35'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the "search" service in the hotel-reservation namespace isn\'t running. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments and found that the search deployment has 0/1 pods ready. They looked at the pod details and found no pods exist. Then they checked the deployment\'s spec and found the image is yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest. They also checked the secrets in the namespace but found none.\n\nHmm, the image is from a private registry, but there are no secrets. That probably means the deployment can\'t pull the image because it doesn\'t have the necessary credentials. But the user wants to confirm this. The next step is to check the deployment\'s events to see if there are any error messages related to pulling the image. \n\nThe command to describe the deployment should show events. If the image pull is failing, the events would mention something like "Failed to pull image" or "ImagePullBackOff". That would confirm the root cause. So the user should run kubectl describe deployment search -n hotel-reservation to check the events. \n\nWait, they already ran that command earlier. Let me check the previous steps. Oh, the user ran kubectl describe deployment search and saw the events. The events might have indicated an image pull error. But in the response, the events weren\'t shown. Wait, the user\'s last tool call was to get the image, then check secrets, then describe the deployment again. \n\nWait, the user\'s last tool call was to describe the deployment, and the response might have included events. But in the provided response, the tool_response was empty. So maybe the user needs to check the events again. Alternatively, maybe the image is invalid or the registry is unreachable. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the deployment is configured with an image that\'s not accessible. But since there are no secrets, the pull would fail. The root cause is likely the missing secrets for the private registry. But the user needs to confirm this by checking the events. \n\nSo the next step is to check the events in the deployment\'s description. If the events show image pull errors, then the missing secrets are the root cause. If not, maybe there\'s another issue. But given the context, the most likely cause is the missing secrets for the private image.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get events -n hotel-reservation --field-selector involvedObject.name=search"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-913c1cdb3666fe6b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 519, 'prompt_tokens': 4854, 'total_tokens': 5373, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcab-3c7a-7372-982e-4287ed6dbad7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get events -n hotel-reservation --field-selector involvedObject.name=search'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-913c1cdb3666fe6b', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4854, 'output_tokens': 519, 'total_tokens': 5373}), ToolMessage(content='LAST SEEN   TYPE     REASON              OBJECT              MESSAGE\n93s         Normal   ScalingReplicaSet   deployment/search   Scaled up replica set search-5f668579c9 to 1\n', id='c79c41bf-d74e-4267-8a6f-66c3641586ac', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-913c1cdb3666fe6b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9fc0a4e0-34da-4108-84dd-81a1edfab710')]]
